# Content Machine — agent working rules

## Before anything else: read the mailbox

**[docs/handoff.md](../../docs/handoff.md)** — what the other agent just did, what it
left uncommitted, and what it found broken. More than one agent works in this repo and
nothing signals a switch, so this is the only way to know the tree is not yours.
Verify it against `git log <sha>..HEAD --oneline` and `git status --short` before you
trust it, then **write your own slot as your last edit** (rule 15).

Read [CLAUDE.md](../../CLAUDE.md) for what this project is, its entry points, the
signal contract and the LLM router. This file is about **how to work here**, not what
the code does. It exists because a specific class of defect keeps reaching `main`.

## The one failure mode that matters

**Every defect found in the last three audit passes was green in CI.** Not one was a
crash, a lint error, or a failing test. They were features that reported healthy and
did nothing:

| Shipped | What CI saw | What was true |
|---|---|---|
| `#292` quiet-hours toast DND | test passed | `quiet_hours_reason()` was called with no channel, resolved to `default`, which has no `quiet_hours` block — **muted nothing, ever** |
| `_sources.md` vault template | test passed | template said `source_url:`, the parser reads `source:` — **every note lost its URL** |
| Claim verifier "12/12 backed" | run graded **A** | 7 of 12 claims had been hedged to "reports claim…" by a rewrite pass; only the post-rewrite number was persisted |
| `youtube` signal, Tapology | "no match" for 33 days | both were **blocked**, reporting absence instead of failure |

The DND test **mocked `quiet_hours_reason`** — the exact call that was broken. The
template test asserted `"source_url" in meta` — that a *key existed*, never that the
parser read it. Both tests were sincere and both were structurally incapable of
failing. That is the thing to avoid.

This is the project's own principle, written down in `docs/decisions.md` §18, §24 and
§25. Read those three before adding any gate, check, or repair pass.

## Rules for tests

1. **Never mock the thing you are testing.** If the change is in `X`, the test calls
   `X` for real. Mock the network, the clock, the filesystem, the LLM — never the
   function whose behaviour you are asserting, and never the call that the change
   depends on.
2. **Watch the test fail before you make it pass.** Run it against the unmodified code
   and confirm the failure. Say so in the commit message. A test that was green before
   the fix is not testing the fix.
3. **Assert behaviour, not shape.** Not `assertIn("source_url", meta)` — call the
   parser and assert it returns a URL. Not "the function exists" — call it and check
   what it does with a real input.
4. **Exercise the shipped config** when production reads the shipped config. A fixture
   dict proves your fixture works. `config/channels.json` is what runs.
   `tests/test_validate_channels.py::TestShippedConfigRatchet` is the pattern; it found
   a live gap on its first run.
5. **Reproduce the real case.** If a live run exposed the bug, put that run's actual
   strings in the test. `tests/test_title_grounding.py` uses run 71's real title and
   fact; that is why it is convincing.
6. **When a fix does not cover a case, assert the gap.** Write the test for the
   remaining hole asserting *current* behaviour, with a docstring saying why and what
   would close it (see `test_known_gap_a_shared_token_still_pulls_the_other_subject_in`
   in `tests/test_description_sources.py`). Never let a partial fix read as a whole one.

7. **Ask what else your pattern matches.** A regex, guard or flag that is right for the
   case you meant is not finished. Write the test for the case you did *not* mean to
   catch, then check whether this repo already resolved the same ambiguity — twice now
   it had. `core/spoken_numbers.py` rewrote `25-4` correctly and also turned
   "5-10 years" into "five ten years" and "10-15%" into "ten fifteen%" on every channel;
   `core/fact_grounding.py` had solved that exact case 40 lines away by requiring a verb
   cue. Why: [agent_collaboration.md](../../docs/agent_collaboration.md).
8. **A manifest edit is not an applied upgrade.** Changing a pin in `pyproject.toml`
   changes nothing until something installs it. Verify with the *runtime* version, never
   the file — `pyproject.toml` read `Pillow==11.3.0` while the environment ran 9.5.0,
   so 26 CVEs stayed live and CI diverged from the operator's machine on a major
   version. The same applies to config: check the value the code actually reads.

## Rules for features

9. **A new helper needs a real caller.** Before finishing, trace the path from a
   production entry point (`main.py`, `scripts/ops.py`, the pipeline, a signal) to your
   function. If nothing reaches it, it is not shipped — it is dead code with a test.
10. **Run the thing.** For an operator-facing change, actually invoke it —
   `py -m scripts.ops <verb>`, or the CLI path — and paste what it printed. Several
   "shipped" items have been inert on first real invocation.
11. **Fail-open must be fail-visible** (§24). A swallowed exception logs: `warning` when
   a guarantee is lost, `debug` for best-effort enrichment, or `# noqa: S110` with the
   reason on the line. And a healthy run must emit **zero** new warnings — a warning
   that always fires trains the operator to ignore warnings.
12. **A pass that rewrites its input reports both numbers** (§25). If you repair,
    regenerate, or hedge something a check just judged, persist the pre-repair verdict
    too. Otherwise the fix erases the evidence that there was anything to fix.
13. **Public surfaces get stricter rules than internal ones.** The YouTube title,
    description, and tags are seen by viewers and by YouTube's policy systems. Loose
    internal relevance is a prompt-quality problem; the same looseness in a description
    is a visible error.

## Repo rules that break things when ignored

- **Tests:** `python -m unittest discover -s tests -t .` — the `-t .` is load-bearing.
  Without it `tests/__init__.py` never loads and the suite writes the operator's real
  `data/` files. Write `unittest.TestCase`, not pytest fixtures.
- **Never write real `data/`** — `quota_state.json`, `youtube_quota.json`,
  `cache_stats.json`, `signal_cache.json`, `data/traces/`. Reuse `GovernorCase` or
  `_IsolatedStateCase`. See `tests/CLAUDE.md`.
- **Never write the operator's real Obsidian vault** from a test. Point
  `OBSIDIAN_VAULT_PATH` at a tempdir.
- **Never commit** `.env`, `config/secrets/`, `.vault_backups/`, `.db_backups/`.
- **Signals** must return `make_signal()`'s shape, be thread-safe, and never raise.
  Don't add a second cache inside a signal. See `apis/CLAUDE.md`.
- **Don't touch** budget or circuit-breaker logic (`core/cost_meter.py`,
  `apis/apify_client.py`, `core/quota_governor.py`) without first reading how the
  breaker trips — a wrong change silently disables a paid signal across runs.
- **`ops secrets-doctor` must never print a secret value.**
- **No network in tests.**
- **Sign your commits** — `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>`. Both
  agents commit as the same git author, so this trailer is the only per-commit record
  of who wrote a change. (This reverses an earlier ban, lifted 2026-08-28.) Still no
  "Generated with ..." lines — that is marketing, not provenance.

## Rules for working alongside the other agent

14. **Commit your own work.** Four times now, changes have been left uncommitted for
    someone else to discover, verify and commit — so CI and a fresh clone saw nothing,
    and twice a tree was measured that disagreed with `git log`. If you changed it, you
    commit it. Do not leave a wave in the working tree.
15. **Write your slot in [docs/handoff.md](../../docs/handoff.md) as your last edit.**
    Defects before wins. Overwrite your own slot; never edit the other agent's. A slot
    reconstructed from memory next session is the thing the file exists to replace.
16. **Re-read immediately before editing.** The other agent may be working right now.
    Never `git checkout` a file to discard changes you did not make.
17. **A green suite is not evidence a test can fail.** Three reviews in a row found
    guard tests that could not go red: one asserted token colours were *present* when
    the question was which won the cascade (it passed with the generator deleted); one
    asserted `assertIn("0", qss)`, which matches any string containing a zero. Before
    committing a guard, break the thing it guards and watch it fail. If it stays green,
    it is decoration.
18. **Simulate ubuntu before claiming green.** CI runs `ubuntu-latest` on every job.
    A test asserting a username is absent passed locally only because the developer's
    home directory contained it, and failed on CI. Anything touching `Path.home()`,
    `USERNAME`/`USER`, path separators, or a vault path must be run with those patched
    to non-local values, not just run.
19. **Never ship against a recorded operator decision.** #333's store was specified in
    `planning_log.md` as *"able to VETO (operator wants a hard block, not a warning)"*
    and shipped warn-only, with the reversal recorded nowhere. If a decision looks
    wrong, say so in your slot and leave it open. Shipping the other answer silently
    is the one thing this mailbox cannot recover from.
20. **Say when finished output changes.** #512 baked grain and a vignette into every
    render, on by default, with no kill switch and no mention in the wave notes. The
    same wave changed five safety gates so `yes` overrides where it used to refuse, and
    made `emit()` write ANSI cursor sequences to a TTY — all under a heading promising
    the terminal was byte-identical. Behaviour changes are not free just because a test
    covers them; name them where the operator reads.

## Definition of done

Not "the tests pass" — they passed for every defect in the table above.

- [ ] `ruff check .` and `ruff format --check .` clean
- [ ] `python -m unittest discover -s tests -t .` green, count **increased**
- [ ] The new test **failed** before the change (state it in the commit)
- [ ] A *guard* test was watched going red with the guarded thing broken — "it passed"
      is not evidence a guard works (rule 17)
- [ ] Anything reading `Path.home()`, `USERNAME`/`USER` or a vault path was run with
      those patched to non-local values (rule 18)
- [ ] `mypy` matches the recorded baseline — it is a separate CI job and a new type
      error hides in a green suite
- [ ] Nothing under `data/` changed — `git status --short data/` is empty
- [ ] Every new function is reachable from a production caller
- [ ] Operator-facing changes were actually run, and the output pasted
- [ ] A healthy run emits no new WARNING
- [ ] Your work is **committed**, not left in the working tree (rule 14)
- [ ] Your slot in [docs/handoff.md](../../docs/handoff.md) is rewritten (rule 15)

## Commit messages

Say what was broken and how you know. The message is the audit trail: name the
measurement, not the intention. "Measured: entity grounding returns `[]` for both the
right and wrong actor" beats "improved title checking".
